WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.410 --> 00:00:03.055
<v Examiner>Exam case 3, continued.

2
00:00:03.055 --> 00:00:06.487
<v Examiner>Ane's airway defences, and Ane's blood pressure.

3
00:00:06.937 --> 00:00:07.651
<v Examiner>Three marks.

4
00:00:07.651 --> 00:00:11.996
<v Examiner>How does the body usually keep dust and small particles out of the lungs?

5
00:00:11.996 --> 00:00:13.721
<v Examiner>There are several mechanisms.

6
00:00:14.171 --> 00:00:16.305
The hook: stick and sweep, filter, cough.

7
00:00:16.755 --> 00:00:19.532
Mucus and cilia: the muco-ciliary escalator.

8
00:00:19.532 --> 00:00:24.076
Nasal hairs: a filter that traps particles, which end up stuck in mucus.

9
00:00:24.076 --> 00:00:28.999
And the carina, at the bottom of the trachea, which sets off the cough reflex.

10
00:00:28.999 --> 00:00:32.407
The epiglottis keeps large objects out of the trachea,

11
00:00:32.407 --> 00:00:34.995
but it's no help against small particles.

12
00:00:35.445 --> 00:00:36.079
<v Examiner>Two marks.

13
00:00:36.079 --> 00:00:39.379
<v Examiner>The muco-ciliary escalator is one of the mechanisms.

14
00:00:39.379 --> 00:00:40.458
<v Examiner>How does it work?

15
00:00:40.908 --> 00:00:44.974
Cilia, tiny hair-like structures lining the respiratory epithelium,

16
00:00:44.974 --> 00:00:47.765
beat to sweep mucus up and out of the airways.

17
00:00:47.765 --> 00:00:51.891
And mucus is sticky: dust and particles get stuck in it and trapped,

18
00:00:51.891 --> 00:00:54.561
and the cilia carry them up and out with it.

19
00:00:55.011 --> 00:00:57.715
Her formative test gives it a tag line:

20
00:00:57.715 --> 00:01:03.678
the mechanism formed by the pseudostratified columnar epithelium lining the airways is

21
00:01:03.678 --> 00:01:05.550
the muco-ciliary escalator.

22
00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:06.643
<v Examiner>Two marks.

23
00:01:06.643 --> 00:01:08.636
<v Examiner>Smoking damages this escalator.

24
00:01:08.636 --> 00:01:10.629
<v Examiner>How would this affect a smoker?

25
00:01:11.079 --> 00:01:15.318
The cilia are damaged, so mucus can't be moved up and out of the lungs.

26
00:01:15.318 --> 00:01:19.677
And a smoker's cough develops, to replace the lost function of the cilia.

27
00:01:21.127 --> 00:01:21.809
<v Examiner>Four marks.

28
00:01:21.809 --> 00:01:26.153
<v Examiner>Ane's blood pressure was high because of the fight or flight response.

29
00:01:26.153 --> 00:01:29.876
<v Examiner>What process helps bring it back to normal after the attack?

30
00:01:29.876 --> 00:01:31.303
<v Examiner>Think the full process.

31
00:01:31.753 --> 00:01:33.692
This is the baroreceptor reflex.

32
00:01:33.692 --> 00:01:38.238
Her recorded session talks it through, and she says think the full process,

33
00:01:38.238 --> 00:01:39.390
so give every link.

34
00:01:39.840 --> 00:01:44.226
The hook: stretch, medulla, brake on, accelerator off, vagus slows the heart,

35
00:01:44.226 --> 00:01:45.024
vessels widen.

36
00:01:45.474 --> 00:01:45.723
One.

37
00:01:45.723 --> 00:01:48.023
The carotid and aortic baroreceptors,

38
00:01:48.023 --> 00:01:51.381
which are stretch receptors in the blood vessel walls,

39
00:01:51.381 --> 00:01:53.868
pick up that the blood pressure is high.

40
00:01:53.868 --> 00:01:55.671
The walls are very stretched.

41
00:01:56.121 --> 00:01:56.403
Two.

42
00:01:56.403 --> 00:02:01.687
They send the message to the medulla oblongata, to its cardio-acceleratory,

43
00:02:01.687 --> 00:02:04.505
cardio-inhibitory and vasomotor centres.

44
00:02:04.955 --> 00:02:05.347
Three.

45
00:02:05.347 --> 00:02:08.156
The cardio-inhibitory centre is stimulated,

46
00:02:08.156 --> 00:02:11.292
and the cardio-acceleratory centre is inhibited.

47
00:02:11.292 --> 00:02:15.473
As she puts it, you can't brake and accelerate at the same time.

48
00:02:15.923 --> 00:02:16.231
Four.

49
00:02:16.231 --> 00:02:18.573
The message goes down the vagus nerve.

50
00:02:18.573 --> 00:02:23.133
The rate and the force of the heartbeat drop, so the cardiac output drops.

51
00:02:23.583 --> 00:02:23.920
Five.

52
00:02:23.920 --> 00:02:28.831
The vasomotor centre brings about vasodilation: more space for the blood.

53
00:02:29.281 --> 00:02:33.164
And six: together, that brings the blood pressure back down to normal.

54
00:02:33.614 --> 00:02:34.212
<v Examiner>Once more.

55
00:02:34.212 --> 00:02:36.665
<v Examiner>Ane's defences, and Ane's blood pressure.

56
00:02:39.115 --> 00:02:44.278
The defences: mucus and cilia, the muco-ciliary escalator; nasal hairs that filter;

57
00:02:44.278 --> 00:02:49.442
the carina, which triggers the cough reflex; and the epiglottis, for large objects.

58
00:02:49.442 --> 00:02:53.921
The escalator: cilia on the respiratory epithelium beat to sweep sticky,

59
00:02:53.921 --> 00:02:55.850
dust-trapping mucus up and out.

60
00:02:55.850 --> 00:03:01.262
Smoking damages the cilia, so mucus can't be moved, and a smoker's cough replaces them.

61
00:03:01.262 --> 00:03:06.426
Blood pressure: the carotid and aortic baroreceptors sense the stretch and tell the

62
00:03:06.426 --> 00:03:07.546
medulla oblongata.

63
00:03:07.546 --> 00:03:13.020
The cardio-inhibitory centre is stimulated and the cardio-acceleratory centre inhibited,

64
00:03:13.020 --> 00:03:17.375
and the vagus nerve slows the rate and force, so cardiac output drops.

65
00:03:17.375 --> 00:03:22.165
The vasomotor centre causes vasodilation, and the pressure returns to normal.
